---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description } from '../../content';

const path = '/guides/revenue-attribution';
const title = "Shopify Email Revenue Attribution — Operator Playbook";
const desc = description(site, 'guides/revenue-attribution');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Do UTMs prove incremental revenue?",
    "answer": "No. They label tracked visits and can support reconciliation. They do not establish whether an order would have happened without a message."
  },
  {
    "question": "How long should a holdout run?",
    "answer": "Plan it from event volume, the effect worth detecting and the outcome horizon. Compare mature cohorts and report uncertainty rather than using a universal duration."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is margin-adjusted attribution incremental profit?",
    "answer": "No. It is contribution attached to credited orders. Incremental profit requires an appropriate comparison and incremental costs measured on a compatible basis."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a class="link" href="/guides">← All operator guides</a>
      <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Revenue Attribution</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Separate attributed orders from incremental profit</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Attribution asks which touch receives credit under a reporting rule. Incrementality asks what changed because of the intervention. Keep both in the report, with their units, observation windows and limits, so a flow dashboard does not become an unsupported causal claim.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Write a measurement contract</h2>
      <p>Define the decision: optimize a message, retain a flow, change a discount or justify platform spend. Specify the population, intervention, assignment unit, outcome and observation horizon before collecting results. Name the owner and record the configuration version. Different questions need different comparisons; a subject-line test cannot establish the value of the entire email program.</p>
      <p>Record attribution settings separately: eligible interactions, lookback window, timezone, order date basis, currency and treatment of refunds, tax, shipping and subscription renewals. Export the actual settings instead of assuming all platforms use last click or the same default window.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Build an order-level reconciliation ledger</h2>
      <p>Use stable order identifiers to join order records with flow, campaign, message, channel and timestamp evidence. Preserve raw claims from each source and keep a separate deduplicated order table. Distinguish missing tracking from an order that received no marketing interaction. Reconcile totals using the same date boundary and refund policy.</p>
      <p>Email and SMS reports can claim the same order under their respective rules. A chosen credit hierarchy can prevent double counting in a blended report, but it cannot prove causal channel contribution. UTMs help trace tagged visits; they do not identify every exposure or reveal the counterfactual purchase.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Keep revenue, contribution and costs distinct</h2>
      <p>Choose a consistent revenue basis. If revenue is already net of discounts, do not subtract the discount a second time. Apply refunds and cancellations consistently, then subtract relevant goods, payment, fulfillment and other variable costs to estimate contribution. Identify missing cost fields rather than silently assigning them zero.</p>
      <p>Show attributed contribution as a descriptive measure. Subtracting costs from attributed revenue does not make the result incremental. Keep recurring platform cost, send cost and implementation cost visible with an allocation method; distinguish average allocated cost from the marginal cost relevant to an experiment.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Choose a comparison that answers the question</h2>
      <p>A randomized send-versus-no-send comparison can estimate the effect of the tested flow among eligible people under the test conditions. A message-versus-message comparison estimates a different effect. Excluding only a late discount message does not estimate the effect of the entire cart journey. Name the treatment precisely.</p>
      <p>Plan assignment before exposure, keep it stable, and use a unit that limits contamination, such as the customer where repeat entries are possible. Randomization, uncertainty and adequate sample size matter. Choose sample allocation and duration from expected event volume and a meaningful detectable effect; there is no universal holdout percentage or fixed minimum month.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Protect the experiment from contamination</h2>
      <p>Keep essential service communications outside a promotional holdout design. Document other campaigns, SMS, offers and paid retargeting that may affect both groups. If assignment excludes only email while SMS replaces it, the measured contrast includes that substitution. Decide whether that is the policy you intend to evaluate.</p>
      <p>Log assignment, eligibility and actual exposure separately. Analyze by original assignment for the primary estimate so delivery failures or post-assignment engagement do not redefine the groups. Report missing assignments, leakage and concurrent changes. A broken random split cannot be repaired merely by labeling an export a holdout.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Wait for comparable cohort maturity</h2>
      <p>Anchor follow-up to eligibility or assignment, not only the reporting calendar. A recent entrant has had less time to buy than an early entrant. Compare cohorts after the same planned outcome window, or use a method that explicitly handles incomplete follow-up. Mark immature cohorts as provisional.</p>
      <p>Allow the stated refund or return observation period where contribution is the outcome. Subscription renewal, first purchase and discretionary repeat orders answer different questions; retain separate order types rather than automatically treating every renewal as either incremental or worthless. A reminder could affect renewal behavior, but attribution alone cannot establish that effect.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Calculate effects in compatible units</h2>
      <p>Purchase-rate difference equals treatment purchasers divided by treatment assigned people minus control purchasers divided by control assigned people. State the difference in percentage points. Revenue per assigned person uses total eligible outcome revenue in each arm divided by that arm’s assigned population; subtract those averages for a revenue effect.</p>
      <p>For profit, calculate the difference in contribution per assigned person and subtract incremental intervention costs on the same population basis. Do not multiply a purchase-rate difference by attributed revenue or confuse incremental revenue with margin. Report an uncertainty interval and sample counts with the estimate; a small positive point estimate alone is not proof of profit.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Use observational reports honestly</h2>
      <p>When a suitable experiment is unavailable, describe the result as observational. A before-and-after comparison can reflect seasonality, product availability, acquisition mix or other changes. Matching cohorts can address measured differences but cannot guarantee removal of unmeasured confounding. A shorter attribution window changes credited revenue, not the causal effect.</p>
      <p>Use descriptive reports to find broken links, unusual refunds, declining contribution or candidate flows to investigate. Avoid ranking welcome, cart and winback solely on gross attributed totals: their eligibility and purchase intent differ. Relative flow comparisons still need comparable definitions and an explicit decision purpose.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Rehearse the reporting pipeline</h2>
      <p>Proposed exercise: create non-sending fixture records for an order claimed by email and SMS, a refund, a subscription renewal, a late order and a new entrant with incomplete follow-up. Trace each from raw exports to the final report. Verify that a deduplicated total counts an order once and a refund changes the intended measure.</p>
      <p>Check the experiment calculation with unequal group sizes and a person who never received an assigned message. Preserve the expected and actual outputs. These fixtures validate the reporting logic; they are not merchant performance results, a production experiment or evidence of platform revenue lift.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Publish a report that preserves uncertainty</h2>
      <p>Include the scope, configuration dates, attributed revenue, deduplicated revenue, contribution basis, assigned counts, follow-up maturity and any experimental estimate with uncertainty. Separate tested outcomes from forecasts. Explain exclusions and missing data in plain language. Maintain a change log when windows or joins change so trends remain interpretable.</p>
      <p>Choose the next action from the evidence: repair tracking, extend observation, investigate margin or run a better comparison. Avoid automatic retirement after an arbitrary number of tests. Decisions depend on effect size, uncertainty, costs and the purpose of the flow, including customer service value that a purchase-only measure may miss.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><nav aria-label="Related operator guides" class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
    <a class="link" href="/guides/segmentation">Eligibility definitions →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/discount-discipline">Offer governance →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">Platform and send costs →</a>
  </nav></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Revenue attribution FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
